@page "/component-audits"
<PageTitle>Component audits · Blazor Accessibility Toolkit</PageTitle>

<div class="page-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">Component tests · bUnit</p>
    <h1>Component audits</h1>
    <p class="lead">
        <code>AccessibilityFixture</code> renders a component with bUnit and audits the markup it produces. Without a
        browser an audit takes about 2 ms, so you can check every component, with every parameter combination, in every
        state. Add a browser engine and the same markup is audited by axe-core, with your CSS.
    </p>
</div>

<CodeBlock Caption="The shape of every component audit" Code="@Shape" />

<section aria-labelledby="attribution">
    <h2 id="attribution">Findings name the component</h2>
    <p>
        Browser scanners report a CSS selector. In a component-based UI that is not enough: the same markup comes from
        many places. The toolkit uses bUnit's render tree to say which Razor component rendered the element, from the
        root of the fixture to the innermost component. Run the first test and look for <strong>Rendered by</strong>.
    </p>
    <ExampleCard Title="A toolbar with one legacy button" Defect="true" Example="ComponentAuditExamples.LegacyToolbarAudit"
                 Sources="@(["LegacyFormattingToolbar.razor", "UnlabelledIconButton.razor"])">
        <ChildContent>
            <p>The toolbar reuses the old <code>UnlabelledIconButton</code> for its link button. Tab to the last button: it has no name.</p>
        </ChildContent>
        <Stage><LegacyFormattingToolbar /></Stage>
    </ExampleCard>
    <ExampleCard Title="The toolbar with labelled buttons" Example="ComponentAuditExamples.ToolbarAudit" Sources="@(["FormattingToolbar.razor", "IconButton.razor"])">
        <Stage><FormattingToolbar /></Stage>
    </ExampleCard>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="axe-core-heading" id="axe-core">
    <h2 id="axe-core-heading">Add axe-core for the full rule set</h2>
    <p>
        Without a browser, the toolkit runs its Razor rules plus a conservative subset of name, label and ARIA checks. With
        <code>PlaywrightMarkupAuditEngine</code>, the markup is loaded into a pooled browser page and audited by axe-core
        4.14 against WCAG 2.2 A and AA, including color contrast when you supply your stylesheets. The findings are still
        attributed to Razor components.
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="data">
            <thead>
                <tr><th scope="col">Check</th><th scope="col">Without a browser</th><th scope="col">With axe-core</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>Blazor event handlers without keyboard support</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Validation messages, broken and duplicate ids, radio groups, fieldsets</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Names, roles, ARIA validity and structure</td><td>a fallback subset</td><td>all axe-core WCAG rules</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Color contrast</td><td>no</td><td>yes, with your CSS</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Keyboard behavior, focus, scripts</td><td>no</td><td>no: use the <a href="keyboard">interactive host</a></td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
    <ExampleCard Title="The same toolbar, audited by axe-core" Defect="true" Example="ComponentAuditExamples.LegacyToolbarAxeAudit">
        <ChildContent>
            <p>The defect is now reported by axe-core's <code>button-name</code> rule, still attributed to <code>UnlabelledIconButton</code>.</p>
        </ChildContent>
    </ExampleCard>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="states">
    <h2 id="states">Audit every state</h2>
    <p>
        Problems often appear only after an interaction: an expanded panel, an opened dialog, a form after a failed
        submit. Change the state with bUnit and audit again. Each audit carries the state in its label, in reports and
        in the baseline.
    </p>
    <ExampleCard Title="A disclosure, collapsed and expanded" Example="ComponentAuditExamples.DisclosureStatesAudit" Sources="@(["DisclosureCard.razor"])">
        <Stage><DisclosureCard Title="Shipping" Details="Ships in 2 days." /></Stage>
    </ExampleCard>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="regressions">
    <h2 id="regressions">Keep known defects detected</h2>
    <p>
        Not every defect is fixed right away. A regression test asserts that a known defect is <em>still reported</em>, so
        that a change to the test setup cannot silently hide it. When the component is fixed, this test fails and reminds
        you to update it and the baseline.
    </p>
    <ExampleCard Title="A regression test for a known defect" Defect="true" Example="ComponentAuditExamples.KnownDefectRegression">
        <ChildContent>
            <p>This test passes because the defect is detected.</p>
        </ChildContent>
    </ExampleCard>
</section>

@code {
    private const string Shape = """
        await using var fixture = await AccessibilityFixture.For<SettingsPanel>()
            .WithParameter(p => p.Title, "Settings")           // parameters, child content, cascading values,
            .WithServices(s => s.AddSingleton(clock))          // services, authorization, bUnit context ...
            .WithSettings(settings)                            // optional: a browser engine and your CSS
            .BuildAsync();

        var audit = await fixture.AuditAsync("initial");
        audit.ShouldHaveNoViolations();                        // or ShouldHaveFinding("rule-id") for a known defect
        """;
}
